# SYNOPSIS

```usage
inputd
```

# DESCRIPTION

`inputd` holds a session of the input class protocol (docs/proto/input.md) with each input driver, and serves their records as text on `/srv/input`, which the shell's and POSIX namespaces union into `/dev`.
svcd(8) gives it a connector to each post an input driver may serve (`input0` to `input3`); it asks each for a session at once, and a post with no driver never answers.

It keeps the last 1024 records of every device.
Opening an events file starts at the oldest it keeps; a read returns whole lines, and waits for the next record once it has read them all.

Its one client, winsrv(8), asks on `/srv/input` for a session with every device's records in binary, each tagged with its post number (docs/proto/input.md §3a); while it holds the input, the console gets no keys.
Without a client, it types the keyboards' keys into the console through its `kbdin` (drv-uart-16550(3)): the rune each key makes with the modifiers held, in the US layout, so a machine with no serial line can type into rc.

# FILES

: `/dev/input/N/info`
  The device on post N as an ndb(6) record: `name`, `kind` (`keyboard` or `pointer`), and a pointer's `absolute`, `relative` and `wheel` and an absolute one's `x_max` and `y_max`.
: `/dev/input/N/events`
  Its records, a line each.
: `/dev/input/keyboard`, `/dev/input/pointer`
  Every keyboard's records, and every pointer's, in one stream each.

A key's line has the device, the time, the HID usage (page:id, in hex), the action (`up`, `down` or `repeat`), the unmodified rune (`rune=`, if it has one), the modifiers and the held set:

```text
key dev=0 t=1328060669 usage=7:17 down rune=t mods=shift held=7:e1,7:17
```

A pointer's has the absolute position, the relative motion, the wheels and the buttons held (bit n-1 for button n):

```text
pointer dev=1 t=2128722722 x=16384 y=8192 dx=0 dy=0 wheel=0 hwheel=0 buttons=0
```

